# Studio — environment template
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM THE ROOT .env
# ---------------------------------------
# Bun loads the root `.env` into anything it runs as code, but it does NOT expand it inside
# `package.json` script strings — and `dev` here is exactly that:
#
#   "dev": "NEXT_PUBLIC_ENKAKU_CORE_URL=${...:-http://localhost:7700} next dev -p 3001"
#
# That `${...}` is resolved by a shell which never saw the root file, so a value set there
# would be silently ignored and you would spend a while wondering why. Next.js loads a `.env`
# from its own directory, which is this one — so Studio's variables live here.
#
# Everything is optional; `bun run dev:studio` works with this file absent.


# Where Studio talks to the core. The default matches `bun run dev`, so set this only when
# the core is somewhere else — a remote farm, or a non-default port.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefixed variables are inlined into the browser bundle at build time. Never
# put a secret in one: it ships to every visitor.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENKAKU_CORE_URL=http://localhost:7700
